// Cheat sheet — phrased to be said aloud
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

window.CHEAT = [
  ['Architecture in 30 seconds', 'My app is FastAPI with Postgres, and Groq for parsing. Metrics: Prometheus pulls /metrics from the app and Node Exporter every 15 seconds, keeps 15 days, and Grafana draws three dashboards that live as JSON in the repo. Logs: the app prints JSON lines to stdout, Docker saves them, Filebeat parses them into fields and sends them to Elasticsearch, which keeps 7 days, and I search them in Kibana. The app doesn\'t depend on any of the monitoring, so if monitoring breaks, the app keeps working.'],
  ['Metrics in 60 seconds', 'There are 19 metrics, all declared in metrics.py, covering all four types. Counters only go up, like gym_http_requests_total for every response, or gym_sets_written_total for sets saved. Gauges go up and down, like gym_review_drafts_open, the parsed entries waiting for review. The latency metric gym_http_request_duration_seconds is a histogram, so I get p95 and p99 with histogram_quantile over a five-minute window. Summaries like gym_sets_per_entry only give sum and count in the Python client, so I show them as averages. Labels are always small fixed sets: route template, method, status, outcome — never user ids or request ids.'],
  ['Logs in 60 seconds', 'Every event is one JSON line with Elastic Common Schema names. The formatter adds the timestamp, level, service name and the request id, which lives in a contextvar set by the middleware, so every line from one request shares it. The main log is "request served" from the middleware with method, path, status and duration. Errors get "request failed" with the exception type and stack. I log IDs and counts, never journal text or secrets, and a filter plus the formatter redact anything that slips through.'],
  ['Metric trace in 30 seconds', 'On save, app.py calls sets_written_total.inc(inserted_sets). That\'s just a number in memory. /metrics prints it as text, Prometheus scrapes it every 15 seconds and stores 0, then 17, then 0 after a restart, then 16. Grafana runs increase over 24 hours, which treats the drop as a counter reset and adds both rises: 33.'],
  ['Log trace in 30 seconds', 'The middleware logs "request served". The formatter turns it into one JSON line on stdout. Docker wraps it in its own envelope in a file. Filebeat strips the envelope and decode_json_fields parses our JSON into nested fields. Elasticsearch stores the status as a long, the duration as a float and the request id as a keyword, so in Kibana http.request.id: "259fd665642941e0" finds exactly that request\'s two lines.'],
  ['Fault experiment in 60 seconds', 'I delayed every fifth request by 500 milliseconds using environment variables, with asyncio.sleep inside the timed part of the middleware. I predicted p95 would rise to about 500 ms, p50 wouldn\'t move, no errors, throughput down. Result: p50 2.5 to 3.3 ms, p95 4.8 to 687.5 ms, p99 to 737.5, 118 faults with 118 warning logs, zero errors, throughput down 32%. p95 moved because 20% of requests were slow, so the 95th percentile falls in the slow group while the median doesn\'t. It\'s 687 not 500 because histogram_quantile interpolates inside the 0.5 to 0.75 second bucket. An error-rate alert would have stayed green. After removing the fault, p95 went back to 5.6 ms.'],
  ['Cardinality experiment in 60 seconds', 'Prometheus stores one time series per unique set of label values. In a separate demo app I labelled a counter with request_id: 1, 10, 25, 50, 100 unique ids gave 1, 10, 25, 50, 100 series, and it held at 100 because of the cap. The same counter labelled only by endpoint stayed at one series. Restarting without the label stopped the growth, but the old series stay in Prometheus until the 15-day retention clears them. At 100 requests a second that would be 8.64 million new series a day. So request ids go in logs, which are built for finding one event, and a test fails if anyone adds an id-like label.'],
  ['Three metrics I should definitely know', '1) gym_http_request_duration_seconds — Histogram, seconds, labels method+route, observed in the middleware, p95 via histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le) (rate(…_bucket[5m]))). 2) gym_sets_written_total — Counter, sets, no labels, .inc(inserted_sets) on save, increase([24h]). 3) gym_review_drafts_open — Gauge, drafts, no labels, DraftTracker dict with 30-min TTL, opened at /log, closed at /save.'],
  ['Two logs I should definitely know', '1) "request served" — info, middleware, method/url.path/http.route/status/event.duration_ms + request id. 2) "request failed" — error, logger.exception in the middleware\'s except, adds error.type/message/stack_trace, status 500.'],
  ['Most important files', 'metrics.py, logging_setup.py, app.py (log_requests middleware and POST /save), faults.py, observability/cardinality_demo.py, docker-compose.yml, observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml, observability/filebeat/filebeat.yml, observability/grafana/dashboards/01-app-performance.json.'],
  ['Most likely professor questions', 'Why is latency a histogram? · Why 33 and not 16? · How does a log get to Kibana? · Why is p95 687 and not 500? · Why not request_id as a label? · What happens if Prometheus / Elasticsearch / Filebeat dies? · down vs down -v? · Where is sensitive data filtered?'],
  ['Things I currently don\'t understand (from the report — be honest)', 'How histogram_quantile behaves when a value lands exactly on a bucket boundary (answer: buckets are le, "less than or equal", so it counts in that bucket); the exact point a log waiting in Filebeat is lost to Docker rotation (when the rotated file is deleted before Filebeat finished it); how to estimate Prometheus memory from series count (rule of thumb: a few KB per active series in the head block).'],
];
